# Pinned tool versions for the metacraft Windows dev environment.
# Shared install root: WINDOWS_DIY_INSTALL_ROOT (default D:\metacraft-dev-deps)

# M2 reconciliation (2026-05-31): JUST/GH/MAVEN pins bumped to catalog HEAD
# per the M2 decision table (bump where the pin had no fixture
# dependency and no behavioural break is expected). GRADLE/ZIG stayed
# on the existing pin and the catalog grew an extra slice via the M66
# harvester (gradle: 8.x LTS preservation; zig: M44 fixture pin to
# 0.13.0). SWIFT is an M70-deferred-8 tool whose migrator outcome is
# "TODO" regardless of the catalog slice set, so M2 left its env.ps1
# pin as-is — the canonical M2 reconciliation closure lives in M3-M5
# (cakBuiltin realize-time hooks).
#
# JUST/GH/PYTHON/GIT_REPO pins live in repo-workspaces/windows/toolchain-
# versions.env now (the framework env.ps1 owns those installs).
VULKAN_HEADERS_VERSION=1.3.296.0
MESA_VERSION=24.3.4
# M49: JDK + Maven (Adoptium Temurin LTS + Apache Maven 3.9.x)
JDK_VERSION=21.0.5
JDK_BUILD=11
MAVEN_VERSION=3.9.16
# M50: Gradle 8.x (JDK 21 from M49 is a hard runtime prerequisite).
# M2 left the env.ps1 pin at 8.10.2 (Gradle 8.x LTS); the catalog
# carries 9.5.1 as HEAD and 8.10.2 as the M2 backfilled slice so
# operators on a migrated home.nim resolve cleanly without forcing a
# major-version bump.
GRADLE_VERSION=8.10.2
# M51: Swift toolchain (swift.org Windows installer)
SWIFT_VERSION=5.10.1
# M52: OCaml + Dune. OCaml ships via MSYS2's mingw64 pacman package
# (``mingw-w64-x86_64-ocaml``); MSYS2 does not package dune, so
# ensure-ocaml.ps1 bootstraps it from the upstream source tarball
# (``ocaml boot/bootstrap.ml`` against github.com/ocaml/dune/releases).
# No version pin on ocaml itself — MSYS2 ships current stable
# (5.x as of 2026); dune is pinned to 3.23.1 inside ensure-ocaml.ps1.
# M53: Zig 0.13.0 (pre-1.0 pin — bumps require M44 fixture audit)
ZIG_VERSION=0.13.0
# M71: Go toolchain. Drives the reprobuild tests that shell out to
# ``go`` (cmake build_test_helper + the m9 sibling-build flows). Pin
# is a current 1.23.x — bumps within the same minor are safe.
GO_VERSION=1.23.4
# Free Pascal Compiler. Required by the pascal/sudoku_solver fixture
# in _lang_survey/run.sh (and the MCR cascade peeler tests that build
# on top of it). FPC ships as a single Inno Setup installer that
# bundles both the i386 host compiler (ppc386) and the x86_64 cross
# compiler (ppcrossx64) — both live under bin/i386-win32/; the driver
# (``fpc.exe``) picks the right backend via ``-P<cpu>``. Pin to the
# 3.2.2 release; bumps require re-validating the survey fixture.
FPC_VERSION=3.2.2
# clingo — the ASP solver `repro_solver` dlopens at module-init time. This is
# NOT an optional convention toolchain like the pins above: without
# `clingo.dll` on the DLL search path, `repro.exe` and every helper binary
# reprobuild compiles for itself abort before `main`. windows/ensure-clingo.ps1
# installs it from the conda-forge `win-64` package (potassco ships no prebuilt
# Windows binaries; the PyPI wheel hides the C library inside a .pyd).
#
# The build string is part of the asset name and therefore part of the pin.
# To bump: download the new .conda asset from
# https://anaconda.org/conda-forge/clingo/<ver>/download/win-64/<asset>
# and record `sha256sum` of the file here. Keep in sync with the clingo
# resource in infra/machines/server/_windows-runner-001/system_windows_runner.nim.
CLINGO_VERSION=5.8.0
CLINGO_BUILD_STRING=py312he3f8637_1
CLINGO_SHA256=a9e5eb699dd8de3dcc555c28f47a46ca0b3005f784f76aadf70f47267e5afee9
# nim-bearssl — a HARD build dependency of `repro` itself
# (`repro_peer_cache/auth.nim` imports `bearssl/ec`), not an optional
# subsystem. Linux/macOS get it from the flake's `bearssl-src` input;
# windows/ensure-nim-bearssl.ps1 is the Windows counterpart.
#
# This rev MUST stay in sync with the `bearssl-src` input in flake.nix. A
# Windows-only pin would silently fork the dependency across platforms — the
# package is generated bindings over a vendored C tree, so drift surfaces as a
# link error or an ABI mismatch at runtime rather than a clean version clash.
# To bump: change both this line and flake.nix's `rev=` together.
NIM_BEARSSL_REV=9a4eed052abbded2d94feaf3f5bbd95a30ec4671
# OpenSSL — the LINK-TIME dependency of every `--define:ssl` entrypoint
# (`repro`, `repro-binary-cache`, `repro-harvest-apt`). This is NOT the same
# thing as the `openssl` CLI in the builtin catalog
# (packages/openssl.nim), which is a nix-only entry providing `bin/openssl`;
# a linker needs `libssl.dll.a` + headers, which that entry never had.
#
# Why this pin exists at all: the catalog's nim typed-tool emits the PORTABLE
# linker names `-lssl -lcrypto` for any ssl entrypoint
# (packages/nim.nim, `opensslPassLForSsl`) and deliberately does NOT bake a
# search path into them — `t_nim_ssl_dependency.nim` asserts that no ambient
# path leaks into `passL`. Supplying the search directory is therefore the
# TOOLCHAIN ENVIRONMENT's job. On Linux/macOS the flake devShell does it via
# `NIX_LDFLAGS`; on Windows nothing did, so all three ssl entrypoints failed
# with `ld.exe: cannot find -lssl`. windows/ensure-openssl.ps1 is the Windows
# counterpart of that flake input, and env.ps1 exports its lib dir through
# `LIBRARY_PATH` (which gcc consults for `-l` lookup natively) rather than
# through a `--passL`, so the catalog stays hermetic.
#
# The package is MSYS2's mingw-w64 build for the UCRT runtime. It must be the
# `ucrt64` variant, not `mingw64`: the gcc this repo pins is WinLibs POSIX
# **UCRT** (GCC_VERSION above), and an MSVCRT-linked libcrypto against a UCRT
# gcc is exactly the kind of mismatch that links and then misbehaves at
# runtime. The release suffix (`-1`) is part of the asset name and so part of
# the pin. To bump: pick a new asset from https://repo.msys2.org/mingw/ucrt64/
# and record its `sha256sum` here.
OPENSSL_VERSION=3.6.3
OPENSSL_MSYS2_RELEASE=1
OPENSSL_SHA256=6cab3e35b2b05da3a34126f3fdb7a4d0c942842c8cde355f53a07a717684313a

# ── cold-start toolchain (windows/bootstrap-toolchain.ps1) ──────────────
# nim + gcc pins for the pre-repro bootstrap, moved here with the
# ensure-*.ps1 scripts from the archived repo-workspaces framework.
# The authority for these tools once a repro exists is the builtin
# catalog (libs/repro_dsl_stdlib/.../packages/nim.nim and
# gcc_winlibs.nim); these lines only serve the cold start, so a
# small version skew against the catalog is expected and harmless.
NIM_VERSION=2.2.8
NIM_WIN_X64_SHA256=11fe2415a64a791b899cc78e2eeacdde93b5f122f2fabc447db36d38002bfb8c
NIM_SOURCE_REPO=https://github.com/nim-lang/Nim.git
NIM_SOURCE_REF=v2.2.8
NIM_CSOURCES_REPO=https://github.com/nim-lang/csources_v3.git
NIM_CSOURCES_REF=master
# gcc -- a HARD dependency of every `nim c` invocation, and therefore of the
# whole build, pinned the same way nim/clingo/openssl are: version + release
# tag + archive sha256, fetched from a fixed URL and verified before anything
# is extracted. On Linux/macOS the flake devShell supplies the C compiler;
# these three lines are that guarantee's Windows counterpart.
#
# This used to be a lone GCC_VERSION line serviced by `winget install
# BrechtSanders.WinLibs.POSIX.UCRT`, which was not a pin at all -- winget takes
# no version argument, so the number named a directory and nothing else (an
# observed host had GCC_VERSION=15.2.0 over a compiler reporting 16.1.0), and
# on any host without winget (an ephemeral CI runner has no App Installer) the
# step could not provision at all. ensure-gcc.ps1 now downloads the release
# below and verifies both its sha256 and the version the installed binary
# itself reports.
#
# The release tag encodes the ABI-critical part of the choice and
# ensure-gcc.ps1 parses it: it MUST be the `posix`/`ucrt` variant. The pinned
# OpenSSL is the MSYS2 ucrt64 build, and an MSVCRT gcc against a UCRT
# libcrypto links cleanly and then misbehaves at runtime around FILE*, errno
# and locale.
#
# To bump: pick a release from
# https://github.com/brechtsanders/winlibs_mingw/releases, set GCC_VERSION to
# the gcc version it carries, and record the sha256 published beside the
# x86_64 .zip asset. Keep in step with the builtin catalog's gcc-winlibs entry
# (packages/gcc_winlibs.nim), which pins the same release declaratively for the
# post-bootstrap path.
GCC_VERSION=16.1.0
GCC_WINLIBS_RELEASE=16.1.0posix-14.0.0-ucrt-r2
GCC_WINLIBS_SHA256=78eff1e2e804b6a6320c713f084b8f820c662104a24cea6a3bfcab82032bdd60
